  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Using the fact that $-\\frac{11\\pi }{12}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{5\\pi }{4},$\ncalculate the **exact** value of $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(-\\frac{11\\pi }{12}).$\n\n$\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(-\\frac{11\\pi }{12})=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(x-y)=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $y=\\frac{5\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(-\\frac{11\\pi }{12}) & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{6}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
